.TH QGA9 8
.SH NAME
qga9, qga9-start \- QEMU guest management for 9front
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B aux/qga9
.B -d
.I device
[
.B -t
.I uart|stream
]
[
.B -c
.I controlfile
]
[
.B -b
.I baud
]
[
.B -S
]
[
.B -D
]
.PP
.B aux/qga9 -s
.PP
.B qga9-start
[
.I config
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Qga9
serves the QEMU Guest Agent (QGA) JSON protocol on one full-duplex native
device. It has no protocol authentication: access to the host channel is
management authority over the guest under the agent's identity.
Only one agent should own a channel.
.PP
.B -t uart
(the default) configures
.I controlfile
or, by default,
.I device
with
.B ctl
appended. UART settings are 8-bit, no parity, one stop bit and no flow control.
The default baud is 38400; accepted rates are 9600, 19200, 38400, 57600 and 115200.
.B -t stream
opens a binary stream without a control device; it rejects
.B -c
and
.BR -b .
Use it with the separate virtio-serial driver's
.BR #Y0/data .
.PP
.B -D
detaches after validating and opening the channel, copying the descriptor,
namespace, environment and note groups. The child closes inherited descriptors
other than standard input/output/error and the channel/control descriptors.
Foreground agents also take a private namespace for file operations.
Start services in a clean native namespace, not a desktop export namespace.
.PP
.B -S
enables
.BR guest-shutdown .
The default is disabled. Supported modes are powerdown and reboot, using fixed
.B fshalt
or
.B fshalt -r
arguments. Success has no protocol response and does not prove poweroff.
.PP
.B -s
uses standard input/output for diagnostics. It enables queries and file
operations but not execution, clock mutation or shutdown. It is not a safe
unprivileged parser-only sandbox.
.SS Commands and limits
Enabled commands are reported by
.BR guest-info .
The implementation provides sync, delimited sync, ping, OS/host/time metadata,
interfaces, vCPUs, timezone, routes, disks, load estimates, six file operations, clock setting,
execution and execution status. Unsupported QGA commands return errors;
filesystem freeze, trim, password mutation and CPU/memory reconfiguration
are not implemented.
.PP
Requests are limited to 8192 bytes, 512 JSON tokens and depth 16;
responses to 65536 bytes. IDs retain exact numeric lexemes.
Base64 conversion uses libc with strict canonical validation.
File transfers have 16 handles and 4096-byte chunks. Supported file modes
are r, rb, r+, r+b, rb+, w, wb, w+, w+b and wb+; append modes are unsupported.
Paths must be absolute and fit in 1024 bytes including the terminator.
Handles are never reused within one agent instance. Restart invalidates them.
.PP
Existing files are checked through the opened descriptor before truncation.
Only ordinary mounted filesystem files (device type M) are accepted.
Creating a missing file also requires a readable mounted parent directory;
direct device parents such as /env and /srv are rejected before creation.
Creation is exclusive. A private helper anchors its cwd, rejects final-parent
unions using native MCREATE self-bind validation and rechecks the mounted
directory before creating the relative leaf. A leased owner-only srv board
passes the descriptor back without sharing descriptor groups.
Native regression remains pending. Mounted fileservers and metadata changes
are not confined by these checks; later failure is not rollback.
Flush means there is no userspace buffer; it does not guarantee durable storage.
.PP
If its private sampler starts, guest-get-load estimates runnable counts over
one, five and fifteen minutes. It starts at zero with the sampler, not boot
history. Snapshots and fixed-point rounding are approximate; invalid sources
or long sampling gaps reset estimation and fail the query. This sampler has
not yet passed a native guest test.
.PP
Execution has eight job/result slots, 4096-byte input and bounded output capture.
Arguments are literal native argv, not interpolated shell text.
There is no execution sandbox or runtime deadline. A host may explicitly
execute a shell. Results are consumed on final status retrieval; unqueried
results occupy slots. Cancellation and descendant cleanup are best effort.
.PP
Clock setting affects system time and UTC RTC. The two updates are not atomic;
an error can report a partially changed clock.
Channel errors terminate the agent without automatic replay or restart.
Host timeouts and errors are not rollback guarantees.
.SS Startup helper
.I Qga9-start
sources trusted rc configuration from
.I config
or
.BR /cfg/$sysname/qga9 .
Required settings are qga9_enabled=yes, qga9_transport=uart or stream,
and qga9_device. Optional qga9_shutdown is yes or no (default no).
The helper uses the daemon's default UART control path and baud rate.
It applies restrictive permissions to
ordinary configured endpoints. Canonical #Y data paths keep fixed driver
permissions; for these it makes up to 30 one-second readiness waits using
existence checks only, without opening or claiming the stream.
It then launches one detached agent. It is not a restart supervisor.
.SH FILES
.TF /cfg/$sysname/qga9
.TP
.B /cfg/$sysname/qga9
Trusted service configuration.
.TP
.B /rc/bin/qga9-start
Installed startup helper.
.TP
.B #c/bintime
System clock.
.TP
.B #r/rtc
UTC real-time clock.
.SH SOURCE
.B src
and
.B scripts/start.rc
in the 9front-qemu-guest-agent source repository.
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR rc (1),
.IR encode (2),
.IR rune (2),
.IR vconsole (3),
.IR fshalt (8)
.SH BUGS
The agent is experimental. QGA provides no guest trust attestation or host
isolation. Blocking device/fileserver I/O can stop progress.
Neither bounded parsing nor a private namespace protects against a hostile
guest kernel, host or another eve process.
